      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\), we need to express \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) in a form that lies within the range \\([0, 2\\pi)\\), since we can use the unit circle to find the sine value. Specifically, we can subtract \\(2\\pi\\) (which is \\(6\\pi/3\\)) from \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) to bring it within the desired range. \n\nFirst, let's subtract \\(2\\pi\\) from \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\):\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3} = \\sin \\left( 3\\pi + \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(3\\pi\\) represents a full rotation (i.e., \\(\\sin(3\\pi + \\theta) = \\sin(\\theta)\\)), we have:\n\\[\n\\sin \\left( 3\\pi + \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right) = \\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{3}.\n\\]\n\nNow we need to find \\(\\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\). The angle \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\) is coterminal with \\(\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\), and it represents an angle in the second quadrant, where the sine value is positive. The reference angle for \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\) is \\(\\pi - \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nKnowing that \\(\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\sin \\left( \\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right) = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3} = \\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
