      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\), we start by reducing the angle to an equivalent angle within the standard range of \\(0\\) to \\(2\\pi\\). Since \\(360^\\circ = 2\\pi\\), we can express \\(\\pi\\) as \\(\\frac{360^\\circ}{180^\\circ} = \\frac{11\\pi}{3} \\times 120^\\circ\\).\n\nSo we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} = 120^\\circ = 360^\\circ \\times \\frac{11}{3}.\n\\]\nTo find an equivalent value within the standard range, we subtract \\(360^\\circ\\) from \\(120^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n120^\\circ - 360^\\circ = -240^\\circ.\n\\]\nSince we need the sine function to be positive in the standard range, we add \\(360^\\circ\\) to \\(-240^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n-240^\\circ + 360^\\circ = 120^\\circ.\n\\]\nThus, \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is equivalent to \\(120^\\circ\\).\n\nThe sine of \\(120^\\circ\\) is the sine of the supplementary angle 120 degrees which is:\n\\[\n\\sin 120^\\circ = \\sin 180^\\circ - \\sin 60^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 60^\\circ) = \\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}.\n\\]",
